---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

// Accuracy on the 20% stratified test split (random_state 42), from the project notebooks.
// Majority class ("high") is 9,872 of 15,929 rows = 62.0%.
const base = 62.0;
const models = [
  { name: 'Logistic regression', acc: 65.03 },
  { name: 'Neural network', acc: 73.85 },
  { name: 'XGBoost', acc: 74.61 },
  { name: 'Random forest (tuned)', acc: 77.15, best: true },
];
---

<Base
  title="Code quality assessor | Moaz Elkhashab"
  description="A course project that predicts a pylint-based quality label for Python functions from 18 structural features, with the 62% majority baseline stated next to every accuracy."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="head grid">
      <!-- TODO(moaz): rewrite the title and lead in your own voice, and say which parts you did. -->
      <h1>Code quality assessor: 77% accuracy, and what it is worth</h1>
      <p class="lead col-text">
        For a machine learning course (CSCI417, Nile University, spring 2025) our team of five scraped Python functions
        from public GitHub repositories, labelled each with pylint, and trained classifiers to predict the label from
        18 structural features. The best model reaches 77.2% accuracy. Always predicting the most common class reaches
        62.0%, so the real gain is 15 points.
      </p>
      <!-- TODO(moaz): add the repo link here once it is public. -->
    </header>

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="res">
      <div class="col-wide">
        <h2 id="res">Results, with the baseline</h2>
        <ul class="bars" aria-label="Test accuracy by model, with the majority-class baseline at 62.0 percent">
          {models.map((m) => (
            <li>
              <span class="n">{m.name}</span>
              <span class="track"><i class:list={{ best: m.best }} style={`width:${m.acc}%`}></i><b style={`inset-inline-start:${base}%`}></b></span>
              <span class="v">{(m.acc + 0.001).toFixed(1)}%</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p class="meta">
          The thin line is the baseline: 62.0%, from always predicting the high-quality class. I report accuracy only,
          with no per-class scores, so I cannot say how well the rare low and mid classes are found.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="data">
      <div class="col-text">
        <h2 id="data">Data and label</h2>
        <p>
          The GitHub API gave function source from five public repositories of exercises and algorithms. Each function
          got 18 features with the ast module and radon: size, arguments, returns, calls, cyclomatic complexity,
          Halstead difficulty and bugs, docstring and comment counts, naming checks and return length. Stubs were dropped
          (under 3 lines, or complexity 1).
        </p>
        <p>
          The label comes from pylint, run on each function alone with the convention, refactor and warning checks
          switched off, so only errors count against the 0 to 10 score. Up to 5 is low, up to 7 mid, above 7 high. After
          removing 837 duplicates the table had 15,929 functions: 4,163 low, 1,894 mid and 9,872 high, of which 8,312
          scored exactly 10.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="col-note">Split: 80/20, stratified, random_state 42.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="models">
      <div class="col-text">
        <h2 id="models">Models</h2>
        <p>
          Logistic regression on scaled features; XGBoost with defaults; a five-hidden-layer network (13,459
          parameters, dropout, early stopping); and a random forest tuned over 36 settings with 3-fold cross-validation
          (best: 200 trees, depth 30, 75.7% in validation, 77.2% on test).
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section grid" aria-labelledby="limits">
      <div class="col-text">
        <h2 id="limits">What this does not show</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>The label measures pylint errors, not quality.</strong> With style checks off, a function scores 10 unless
          pylint finds an error. Running it on one function alone also flags names defined elsewhere in the file.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The split is random across functions.</strong> Functions from the same repository land in both train and
          test, and five educational repositories say little about code in general.
        </p>
        <p>
          So the model predicts what pylint would say about a function of this kind, not how maintainable the code is.
          A fair next test is to hold out whole repositories and report macro-F1.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .head {
    padding-top: 40px;
    row-gap: 24px;
  }
  .head h1 {
    grid-column: 1 / span 10;
    font-size: clamp(36px, 6.5vw, 64px);
    line-height: 1.02;
    max-width: 18ch;
  }
  .lead {
    font-size: 20px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; }
  table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; font-size: 15px; }
  th, td { text-align: start; padding: 10px 12px 10px 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); vertical-align: top; }
  th { color: var(--ink-soft); font-weight: 600; }
  .bars { list-style: none; margin: 18px 0 0; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 8px; max-width: 640px; }
  .bars li { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 150px 1fr 56px; gap: 12px; align-items: center; font-size: 14px; }
  .bars .n { color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .bars .v { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; text-align: end; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .track { position: relative; height: 12px; background: var(--panel); }
  .track i { position: absolute; inset-block: 0; inset-inline-start: 0; background: var(--rule); }
  .track i.best { background: var(--teal); }
  .track b { position: absolute; inset-block: -3px; width: 2px; background: var(--ink); }
  video { width: 100%; max-width: 860px; display: block; border: 1px solid var(--rule); background: var(--panel); }
  .vcap { color: var(--ink-soft); font-size: 14px; margin-top: 10px; }
  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .head h1 { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
  }
</style>
